<div class="sec" id="s2">
  <div class="num">02</div>
  <h2>Before you let it change files</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    These are my four rules. Keep them even when the task looks boring.
  </p>
  <h3>Never paste secrets into a prompt</h3>
  <p>
    No passwords, bank details or other people's private information. No API
    keys either: these are secret codes that let software use an account or paid
    service. Put them only where the service's instructions say, never in a
    conversation or project file.
  </p>
  <h3>Read the plan before you approve</h3>
  <p>
    If it includes deleting files, installing software or going outside the
    project, ask why. You can say no. Instructions in a prompt or in
    <code class="i">CLAUDE.md</code> are not technical restrictions. "Do not modify
    the photos folder" tells Claude what you want; it does not make that folder read-only.
    The agent can still get it wrong.
  </p>
  <h3>Keep work in one project folder</h3>
  <p>
    Use a folder for this website, never your whole Documents or home folder.
    Keep private material out of it. Depending on the service, configuration and
    what the agent accesses, file contents may be sent to the AI provider as
    context for its answer. Not every file read necessarily sends the whole
    file, but do not assume that local files stay local. Check the service's
    data policy. A project folder helps limit the job; it does not guarantee the
    agent cannot reach elsewhere.
  </p>
  <h3>Make mistakes undoable</h3>
  <p>
    Use copies of photos, not your originals. Before changing a page you like,
    copy the whole project folder somewhere outside it. Backups must never be
    inside any agent's working project, even in a subfolder called "backup".
    Never open the backup itself to an agent; make a separate working copy if
    you want to experiment. This helps recovery, but it is not a security
    boundary or protection for everything on your computer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start each change in Claude Code's Plan mode, then review the plan before
    allowing edits. Auto mode can carry out the approved plan with fewer routine
    questions; manual approval is an alternative. Neither replaces checking the
    result. Do not enable bypass permissions. If something feels off, press
    Escape to interrupt Claude Code and ask the person helping you before
    continuing.
  </p>
</div>
